#!/bin/bash
# backup/help.loc - Module-specific help

cat <<'HELP'
Module: backup - restic client on the SOURCE host

  Runs on the machine that HOLDS the files. It encrypts before anything
  leaves the LAN and is the only place the repository passwords exist.

  The other half is 'backup-box', on the machine that STORES the blobs. That
  box runs rest-server, never restic, and holds no key.

  RETURNING COLD? Read BACKUP.md - it is the design document for the whole
  system. Short version:

    - Two different secrets. The rest-server password is HTTP auth and the
      box knows it. The REPOSITORY password encrypts the data and the box
      must never know it. Lose the second and the backups are gone; that is
      the design working, not a bug.
    - One repository per data class: home, mail, media, root. Splitting keeps
      index memory and prune cost off the high-value data - maildirs are
      millions of tiny files.
    - Paths and excludes come from yamc.local/backup/<repo>.{paths,exclude},
      ported from the old diskback script.

SUBCOMMANDS

  setup                  Install restic, write config, install the schedule.
                         Needs the rest-server password the first time.
      yamc -h uranus -e bk_pass='<password>' backup
      yamc -h uranus backup                          # later runs

  init [repo ...]        Create the repositories on the box. Existing ones
                         are left alone.
      yamc -h uranus backup init

  run [repo ...]         Back up now, whether or not the schedule is on.
                         This is how you seed, and how you test.
      yamc -h uranus backup run
      yamc -h uranus backup run home

  check [repo ...]       Verify integrity. Structure only by default.
      yamc -h uranus backup check
      yamc -h uranus -e data=1 backup check          # + a 1/52 data slice

  snapshots [repo ...]   List what is stored.

  status                 Configuration, schedule, and LAST SUCCESS per repo.
                         This is the freshness alarm - see below.

  env <repo>             Print the export lines for driving restic by hand.

  enable / disable       Turn the cron schedule on or off.

THE MASTER SWITCH

  BACKUP_ENABLED in yamc.local/backup/backup.conf. While it is "no", the
  crontab is installed with every entry commented out.

  That is deliberate here: uranus is being built ahead of taking over from
  sun and does not hold the real home directories yet. Backing up an
  incomplete copy over a good one is worse than not backing up.

    yamc -h uranus backup enable      # when uranus is the live source
    yamc -h uranus backup disable

  'backup run' ignores the switch, so you can seed and test meanwhile.

WHY THERE IS NO 'restore'

  Restores are rare, varied and consequential - better done deliberately with
  the real tool than through a wrapper hiding which flags were used. What a
  wrapper usefully saves is retyping the repository URL and password file, so
  'backup env <repo>' prints exactly that and gets out of the way. It also
  lists the commands worth knowing: dump, find, mount, restore.

VERIFICATION IS THE POINT

  An encrypted repository is opaque: a silently failed backup looks exactly
  like a working one. You can no longer eyeball an rsync mirror, so:

    - 'backup status' reports LAST SUCCESS per repository and fails if any
      is stale. Run it from cron and read the mail.
    - 'backup check -e data=1' weekly covers the whole repository in a year
      at about 2% of the cost per run.
    - Do a real restore twice a year. An untested backup is a hope.

  All of this runs on the SOURCE. A backup box must never report its own
  health: a compromised or wedged box is exactly the one that will claim to
  be fine.

PRUNE

  Not a subcommand yet. Retention is recorded per repository in repos.tab,
  but 'forget --prune' cannot run against an append-only server - it needs a
  maintenance window in which append-only is relaxed on the box, then
  restored. Quarterly, deliberate, never automatic: the append-only guarantee
  is worth more than the disk space. See BACKUP.md.

SEE ALSO

  backup-box   the storage side
  BACKUP.md    system design, threat model, runbooks
HELP
